```java
import java.util.*;

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Charlie", "Alice", "Bob");

// BEFORE Java 8 — anonymous class
Collections.sort(names, new Comparator<String>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(String a, String b) {
        return a.compareTo(b);
    }
});

// AFTER Java 8 — lambda
Collections.sort(names, (a, b) -> a.compareTo(b));

// Even shorter — method reference
Collections.sort(names, String::compareTo);

// Lambda syntax patterns:
Runnable r1 = () -> System.out.println("no params");            // no params
java.util.function.Predicate<String> p = s -> s.length() > 5;   // one param — parens optional
java.util.function.BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add = (a, b) -> a + b; // two params

// Block body when you need multiple statements
Runnable r2 = () -> {
    System.out.println("step 1");
    System.out.println("step 2");
};
```

The three forms above all do the same job — the lambda and the method reference simply strip away the anonymous-class noise.

```java
import java.util.function.*;

public class EffectivelyFinalDemo {
    void demo() {

        // WORKS — 'greeting' is never reassigned
        String greeting = "Hello";
        Runnable r1 = () -> System.out.println(greeting); // captures value of greeting

        // WORKS — explicitly final
        final int multiplier = 3;
        Function<Integer, Integer> triple = x -> x * multiplier;

        // BROKEN — 'count' is reassigned
        int count = 0;
        count++;  // this one line makes count NOT effectively final
        // Runnable r2 = () -> System.out.println(count); // COMPILE ERROR

        // WORKAROUND — single-element array (array reference is final, content isn't)
        int[] mutableCount = {0};
        Runnable increment = () -> mutableCount[0]++; // allowed
        increment.run();
        System.out.println(mutableCount[0]); // 1
        // Note: this is a code smell — avoid in multi-threaded code (race condition)
    }
}
```

The array trick compiles because the reference never changes — only its contents do — but reach for it sparingly, never across threads.

```java
import java.util.function.*;

public class LambdaAccessDemo {
    private String name = "Alice";            // instance variable — heap
    private static int callCount = 0;         // static variable — heap

    void showAccess() {
        String localVar = "local";  // local variable — stack, must be effectively final

        Runnable r = () -> {
            // LOCAL — read only, must be effectively final
            System.out.println(localVar);    // OK — reading it
            // localVar = "changed";         // COMPILE ERROR — can't assign

            // INSTANCE — full read/write access
            System.out.println(name);        // OK
            name = "Bob";                    // OK — modifying instance variable

            // STATIC — full read/write access
            System.out.println(callCount);   // OK
            callCount++;                     // OK
        };
        r.run();
    }

    void thisDemo() {
        // 'this' inside a lambda = the enclosing class instance
        Runnable r = () -> System.out.println(this.name); // 'this' = LambdaAccessDemo
        // Unlike anonymous classes, lambdas don't have their own 'this'
    }
}
```

Notice the only thing the lambda cannot do is reassign `localVar` — both heap fields take a write without complaint.
